I recently built a highly available website on AWS. The goal was to put everything I learnt during my AWS re/Start program 
into practice by building projects. I configured an auto scaling group spanning two private subnets in two different availability 
zones, two ALB nodes in the public subnets receiving and directing traffic into the ASG instances, a security group that allows 
traffic from only the ALB into the instances and a VPC S3 endpoint for the instances to fetch Amazon Linux repos from S3 instead 
of using a NAT Gateway to save cost.

I tried testing the connection to the ALB and it timed out, I tried figuring out what went wrong and for two days, everything 
looked fine. The instances were running, health checks passed, so I decided to test the connection in a terminal and I noticed that 
dig ran but curl hung, DNS resolved so the issue was below DNS. I went back to my route table and that is when I saw 0.0.0.0/16 
set as the destination address for the route to the IGW instead of 0.0.0.0/0. After correcting it, the connection to the ALB went 
through.

That is when I realised that cloud resource setups are not about just provisioning resources and calling it a day, it is about how 
they connect together. A small silent error in the connection and everything will look good, instance running and healthy, making 
you wonder for days where the problem might be.
